.\"
.\"---
.TH VBKX 1 "3 October 2026" "VBACKUP @VBACKUP_IDENT@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vbkx \- the stand-alone extractor of VBACKUP savesets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vbkx l
.I saveset
.br
.B vbkx x
.I saveset
.RB [ \-C
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RI [ name ...]
.br
.B vbkx p
.I saveset name
.br
.B vbkx t
.I saveset
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vbkx
reads savesets made by
.BR vbackup (1)
on a machine where VBACKUP is not installed.  It is linked statically.
.TP
.B l
List the files, one line each: time, size, type and mode, name.
.TP
.B x
Extract all files, or the names given; a directory name takes what is
below it.
.B \-C
names the output directory,
.B \-f
overwrites files that are there.
.TP
.B p
Write one file to the standard output.
.TP
.B t
Read the whole saveset and check every checksum.
.PP
Put back: data, holes, mode, times, symbolic and hard links, FIFOs; as
root the owner (by number) and device files.  Not put back: ACLs,
extended attributes, chattr flags.
.PP
Names that lead out of the output directory (".." , a leading "/", a
symbolic link on the way) are refused.  Damage is repaired as far as
the XOR blocks of the saveset allow; every incomplete file and every
file that could not be reached is named.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 \- done; 1 \- something damaged or not done; 2 \- the command or the
saveset cannot be used.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ vbkx l /mnt/usb/home.bck
$ vbkx x /mnt/usb/home.bck -C /tmp/restore home/rrl/notes
$ vbkx p /mnt/usb/home.bck home/rrl/notes/todo.txt | less
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vbackup (1),
.B vbackup /HELP VBKX
.SH AUTHOR
StarLet Squad and Ruslan R. Laishev (AKA: BadAss SysMan).
